Prerequisites for VELERO-Backup
EKS Cluster, AWS, AWS CLI, S3 BUCKET, KUBECTL, 

=>aws sts get-caller-identity
to check user info

Add configuration file 
The Kubernetes configuration file is stored at ~/.kube/config by default.

=> aws eks update-kubeconfig \
   --region ap-south-1 \
   --name dev-eks

Velero has two components:
1. Velero Client (CLI)
2. Velero Server


1. Velero Client (CLI)
The client is the command-line tool you install on:
Bastion Host
EC2 Machine
Admin Laptop
Jenkins Server

2. Velero Server
Installed inside Kubernetes.
Usually in:   Namespace: velero

=> wget https://github.com/vmware-tanzu/velero/releases/latest/download/velero-linux-amd64.tar.gz        ------Download Velero
=> tar -xvf velero-linux-amd64.tar.gz                        ----Extract
=> sudo mv velero-*-linux-amd64/velero /usr/local/bin/       ----Move binary to PATH
=> velero version --client-only                              ----Client:   Version: v1.16.x
=======================================================================================================================================
Create S3 Bucket for Velero Backup

aws s3 mb s3://eks-velero-backup-prod --region ap-south-1

               OR

Enable Versioning (Recommended)
-------------------------------
aws s3api put-bucket-versioning \
  --bucket eks-velero-backup-prod \
  --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled

                 OR

Enable Encryption (Recommended)
-------------------------------
aws s3api put-bucket-encryption \
  --bucket eks-velero-backup-prod \
  --server-side-encryption-configuration '{
    "Rules": [{
      "ApplyServerSideEncryptionByDefault": {
        "SSEAlgorithm": "AES256"
      }
    }]
  }'
========================================================================================================================================
1. Create IAM Policy for Velero S3 Access
and If You Need EBS Snapshot Backups Too (Add EC2 permissions to the policy)

Example policy:
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "VeleroBucketAccess",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject",
        "s3:PutObject",
        "s3:DeleteObject",
        "s3:ListBucket"
      ],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::eks-velero-backup-prod",
        "arn:aws:s3:::eks-velero-backup-prod/*"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

Save as:
velero-s3-policy.json

Create policy:
aws iam create-policy \
  --policy-name VeleroS3Policy \
  --policy-document file://velero-s3-policy.json

2. Create IAM User
aws iam create-user \
  --user-name velero-user

3. Attach Policy to User
aws iam attach-user-policy \
  --user-name velero-user \
  --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:policy/VeleroS3Policy

4. Create Access Key
aws iam create-access-key \
  --user-name velero-user

Output:
{
  "AccessKey": {
    "AccessKeyId": "AKIAxxxxxx",
    "SecretAccessKey": "xxxxxxxxxxxxx"
  }
}

Save these securely.

5. Create Velero Credentials File

cat > credentials-velero <<EOF
[default]
aws_access_key_id=AKIAxxxxxx
aws_secret_access_key=xxxxxxxxxxxxx
EOF


velero install \
  --provider aws \
  --plugins velero/velero-plugin-for-aws:v1.10.0 \
  --bucket eks-velero-backup-prod \
  --backup-location-config region=ap-south-1 \
  --snapshot-location-config region=ap-south-1 \
  --secret-file ./credentials-velero

          OR 
     IRSA method
velero install \ 
--provider aws \ 
--plugins velero/velero-plugin-for-aws:v1.10.0 \ 
--bucket eks-velero-backup \ 
--backup-location-config region=ap-south-1 \ 
--snapshot-location-config region=ap-south-1 \ 
--service-account-name velero

IRSA = IAM Roles for Service Accounts
Velero Pod -> ServiceAccount -> IAM Role ->AWS

"For Velero on EKS, I use IRSA instead of AWS access keys. First, I enable the EKS OIDC provider. 
Then I create an IAM policy with S3 and EBS snapshot permissions and attach it to an IAM role. 
I create a Kubernetes service account named velero and annotate it with the IAM role ARN. 
During installation I use --service-account-name velero --no-secret. When the Velero pod needs AWS access, 
it uses the service account token to assume the IAM role through the EKS OIDC provider and receives temporary credentials from AWS STS. 
This avoids storing access keys and follows AWS security best practices."

command explaination
1.velero install
Installs the Velero server components into the Kubernetes cluster.
Creates the velero namespace.
Deploys Velero pods and controllers.

2. --provider aws
Tells Velero that the cloud provider is AWS.
Velero loads the AWS plugin.
Enables integration with:
S3
EBS Snapshots
IAM


3.  --plugins velero/velero-plugin-for-aws:v1.10.0
What it does: Installs the AWS plugin.
Think of it as: Translator between Velero and AWS
With plugin:
Velero -> AWS Plugin -> AWS APIs

The plugin allows:
Create EBS Snapshot
Upload to S3
Restore from S3
List Backups

Verify:
=>kubectl describe deployment velero -n velero
Look for:
velero-plugin-for-aws

4.--bucket eks-velero-backup
Specifies the S3 bucket where backups will be stored.
Velero saves:
Deployments
Services
ConfigMaps
Secrets
Backup metadata


5. --backup-location-config region=ap-south-1
What it does: Tells Velero:   My S3 bucket is located in ap-south-1

Without region:
Velero may not find the bucket.


6.--snapshot-location-config region=ap-south-1
What it does:  Configures where EBS snapshots are created.

Example:  Pod --> PVC  --> EBS Volume --> Snapshot
When backup runs: velero backup create prod-backup
Velero creates:
snap-12345

in:
AWS
→ EC2
→ Snapshots
→ ap-south-1

Stored as:
VolumeSnapshotLocation
Check:
velero snapshot-location get
Output: default


7. --secret-file ./credentials-velero
What it does: Provides AWS credentials to Velero.

File content:
[default]
aws_access_key_id=AKIAxxxx
aws_secret_access_key=xxxxxxxx

Velero reads this file.
Creates Kubernetes Secret:  kubectl get secret -n velero

Velero uses these credentials to:
Upload backups to S3
Create EBS snapshots
Restore backups
================================================================================
What is a ServiceAccount?
A ServiceAccount is the identity used by a pod inside Kubernetes.
Example:
User Account --> Human Login

ServiceAccount --> Pod Login

When the Velero pod starts: Velero Pod --> ServiceAccount: velero



Why do we specify it?

In EKS with IRSA:

Velero Pod --> ServiceAccount --> IAM Role --> AWS
 
AWS permissions are attached to the ServiceAccount.
So Velero must use the correct ServiceAccount.



Example IRSA Setup

Create ServiceAccount

apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: velero
  namespace: velero

Apply:
kubectl apply -f sa.yaml

Attach IAM Role
kubectl annotate serviceaccount velero \
  -n velero \
  eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn=arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/VeleroBackupRole

Verify:
kubectl describe sa velero -n velero

Output:
Annotations:
  eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn=arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/VeleroBackupRole


Flow like:  Velero Pod --> Uses ServiceAccount: velero --> Assumes IAM Role: VeleroBackupRole --> Accesses S3 and EBS

Verify Which ServiceAccount Velero Uses
kubectl get deployment velero -n velero -o yaml
Look for: spec:  serviceAccountName: velero

"--service-account-name velero tells Velero to run using the Kubernetes ServiceAccount named velero. In EKS, 
this is commonly used with IRSA, where the ServiceAccount is mapped to an IAM role. 
The Velero pod assumes that IAM role and receives temporary AWS credentials to access S3 and create EBS snapshots without storing access keys."
======================================================================================================================================================================================
1. Backup Entire Cluster
velero backup create full-cluster-backup

2. Backup Specific Namespace
velero backup create payment-backup --include-namespaces dev

3.Check
velero backup get

4. Backup Using Labels
Backup resources having label:
velero backup create payment-backup --selector app=payment

Multiple labels:
velero backup create prod-backup --selector app=payment,env=prod

5. Backup Excluding Namespace
velero backup create cluster-backup --exclude-namespaces kube-system

6. Backup Specific Resources
Only deployments:
velero backup create deployment-backup --include-resources deployments

Multiple resources:
velero backup create app-backup --include-resources deployments,services,configmaps,secrets

7.Schedule Specific Namespace
velero schedule create payment-daily --include-namespaces payment --schedule="0 2 * * *"

8. Backup With TTL
Delete backup automatically after 7 days:
velero backup create prod-backup --ttl 168h0m0s             (168 hours = 7 days)

9. Describe Backup
velero backup describe payment-backup

10. View Backup Logs
velero backup logs payment-backup

11. List Backups
velero backup get
NAME                  STATUS
payment-backup        Completed
daily-backup-abc123   Completed



1.List Available Backups
Before restoring:
velero backup get

NAME                 STATUS
prod-backup          Completed
payment-backup       Completed

2. Restore Entire Backup
Restore everything from a backup:
velero restore create --from-backup prod-backup

This restores:
Namespaces
Deployments
Services
ConfigMaps
Secrets
PVCs


3.Restore with Custom Restore Name
velero restore create prod-restore --from-backup prod-backup


4. Restore Specific Namespace
Suppose backup contains:  payment--orders--frontend
Restore only payment:
velero restore create --from-backup prod-backup --include-namespaces payment


5. Restore Namespace to New Namespace
Very common for testing.
Backup contains: payment
Restore as: payment-new

Command:
velero restore create --from-backup payment-backup --namespace-mappings payment:payment-new


6. Restore Specific Resources
Restore only deployments:
velero restore create --from-backup prod-backup --include-resources deployments

Multiple resources:
velero restore create --from-backup prod-backup --include-resources deployments,services


7. Restore Excluding Resources
velero restore create --from-backup prod-backup --exclude-resources secrets
Everything restored except secrets.




